{"maxId":257,"entries":{"chapter-81-study-chronic-obstructive-pulmonary-disease|Chapter 81 Study: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"epidemiology-amp-definitions|Epidemiology & definitions":["u3"],"copy|Hazzard cited an earlier US mortality ranking and projected COPD to rank fifth for disability and third for death worldwide by 2030 (p1235). In CDC's final 2024 US statistics, the broader category chronic lower respiratory diseases, which includes COPD and asthma, ranked fifth.":["u4"],"copy|Global prevalence 10%–12% (up to 384 million cases, 2010 estimate); spirometry-confirmed COPD prevalence 15.4% in US adults ≥65 (p1235).":["u5"],"copy|The chapter projected that people aged 75 or older would account for about half of adults with COPD by 2030 (p1235).":["u6"],"copy|Dyspnea in one-third of older persons; chronic bronchitis in 15%; wheezing in 12% (p1235).":["u7"],"copy|Chronic bronchitis = chronic cough + sputum production for ≥3 months/year for 2 consecutive years (p1239).":["u8"],"copy|Two-thirds of people with chronic-bronchitis symptoms and half with physician-diagnosed emphysema/COPD have normal spirometry — symptoms ≠ airflow obstruction (p1235).":["u9"],"copy|Bronchiectasis present in up to 30% of COPD patients (p1239).":["u10"],"diagnosis-amp-spirometry|Diagnosis & spirometry":["u11"],"copy|Diagnosis needs compatible history plus spirometry-confirmed airflow obstruction (p1242).":["u12"],"copy|In a compatible clinical context, GOLD uses post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.70. Because the fixed ratio does not adjust for age, it can classify more older adults as obstructed than an age-adjusted lower limit of normal. In the older cohort described here, estimates were 37.7% versus 15.4% (p1244, Table 81-5).":["u13"],"copy|Normal aging lung alone (no COPD) already shows ↑chest-wall rigidity, ↓elastic recoil, \"senile emphysema,\" air trapping, and ↑closing volume approaching FRC — the same physiologic changes seen in COPD, just less severe (single vs double arrows) — part of why fixed spirometric cutoffs overdiagnose older adults (Table 81-1, p1238).":["u14"],"copy|Age-adjusted approach (GLI): airflow obstruction = FEV1/FVC Z-score < –1.64 (5th percentile LLN); –2.55 = 0.5th percentile, a severity marker (p1243–1244).":["u15"],"copy|Table 81-5 reproduces older ATS/ERS categories using FEV1 percent predicted: ≥70%, 50–69% and <50% (p1244). The 2022 ERS/ATS interpretive standard grades impairment by z-score. GOLD's airflow-obstruction grades are a separate framework.":["u16"],"copy|PRISm is a post-bronchodilator spirometric pattern with FEV1/FVC ≥0.70 and FEV1 <80% predicted. It can be associated with respiratory symptoms or later airflow obstruction. Demonstrating airway inflammation is not part of its definition (p1244, GOLD).":["u17"],"comorbidity-amp-functional-burden|Comorbidity & functional burden":["u18"],"copy|COPD patients average 3.7 chronic conditions and 35 filled prescriptions/year (p1247).":["u19"],"copy|Skeletal muscle dysfunction in ~30%; sarcopenia in an estimated 22% (up to 63% in nursing-home residents) (p1241).":["u20"],"copy|Older adults with COPD have a twofold increased risk of frailty (p1241).":["u21"],"copy|Postmortem study of COPD-exacerbation deaths: heart failure 37%, pulmonary thromboembolism 21%, pneumonia 28%, respiratory failure 14% (p1247).":["u22"],"pharmacologic-management-stable-copd|Pharmacologic management (stable COPD)":["u23"],"copy|COPD inhaled treatment: offer a short-acting rescue bronchodilator. GOLD 2026 generally favors LABA/LAMA for symptomatic group B or exacerbation-prone group E patients and considers LABA/LAMA/ICS in group E at eosinophils ≥300 cells/μL. Following exacerbations on LABA/LAMA, ICS benefit is more likely as the eosinophil count rises. Concomitant asthma requires an ICS-containing regimen. Table 81-7 gives cells/μL; the p1253 prose prints cells/mL (Table 81-10, p1249–1252; GOLD treatment algorithms).":["u24"],"copy|ICS risks in older adults: 1.2–1.6-fold increased pneumonia risk (highest with older age, frailty, low BMI, high dose, eosinophils <100 cells/μL); ↓ bone mineral density above 1000 mcg/day fluticasone-equivalent; subcapsular cataracts are established with systemic steroids, while causation with ICS is less certain; atypical mycobacterial infection (p1253–1254).":["u25"],"copy|Roflumilast: oral PDE4-inhibitor for severe obstruction + chronic bronchitis + history of exacerbations (p1254).":["u26"],"copy|Clinical phenotypes steer add-on therapy beyond bronchodilators: bronchiectasis → airway-clearance techniques (not ICS-focused); emphysema → consider LVRS/transplant or α1-antiprotease (if AAT-deficient); COPD-asthma overlap/eosinophilic COPD → consider biologics; COPD-OSA overlap → positive airway pressure (Table 81-7, p1246).":["u27"],"copy|Azithromycin 250 mg daily or 3×/week reduces exacerbations in former smokers; watch for QTc, hearing loss, resistance; maintenance may be considered for frequent exacerbators despite maximal triple inhalers when worsening airflow obstruction and airway inflammation drive symptoms, without hearing impairment, resting tachycardia or other arrhythmia, apparent QTc-prolongation risk, or clinical/radiographic NTM evidence, with NTM assessment before regular use (p1254).":["u28"],"copy|Theophylline: goal level 8–12 mcg/mL; narrow therapeutic window, rarely used now (p1255).":["u29"],"devices-amp-adherence|Devices & adherence":["u30"],"copy|DPIs need PIF >45 L/min; SMIs need only a gentle breath — an advantage over DPIs/pMDIs for low-PIF patients (p1255).":["u31"],"copy|Cognitive-impairment cutoffs predicting poor inhaler technique: MMSE <24 or EXIT-25 ≥15 (p1255).":["u32"],"copy|Nebulizers/compressors fall under Medicare Part B; handheld inhalers under Part D — cost-relevant for donut-hole patients (p1255).":["u33"],"acute-exacerbations|Acute exacerbations":["u34"],"copy|Average older COPD patient has 2–3 exacerbations/year, each lasting up to 2 weeks; exacerbations cause up to 20% of hospitalizations in those ≥75 (p1259).":["u35"],"copy|Respiratory infection causes ~70% of exacerbations; bacteria 40–60% (H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, S. pneumoniae most common) (Table 81-12, p1259).":["u36"],"copy|Systemic corticosteroids: delirium risk rises above 60 mg/day prednisone-equivalent; a 5-day course of 40 mg/day prednisone was noninferior to a 7–14-day taper (p1236, p1261).":["u37"],"copy|Antibiotic duration typically 3–7 days (longer, 10–14 days, if bronchiectasis) (p1236, p1261).":["u38"],"copy|NPPV/BPAP for acute COPD exacerbation with acute hypercarbia (PaCO2 >45–50 mm Hg) and respiratory acidosis (no contraindications) reduces intubation, LOS, and mortality (p1257).":["u39"],"surgical-amp-ltot|Surgical & LTOT":["u40"],"copy|LTOT indicated for resting PaO2 ≤55 mm Hg or SpO2 ≤88% (daily use ≥16 h/day improves survival) (Table 81-11, p1256).":["u41"],"copy|LVRS candidates: FEV1 <50% predicted, RV >150–200% predicted, upper-lobe-predominant emphysema; FEV1 or DLCO <20% predicted + homogeneous emphysema = higher surgical mortality, do not operate (p1258).":["u42"],"copy|Lung transplant referral thresholds: both FEV1 and DLCO <25% predicted, PaCO2 >50 mmHg, BODE >5; median post-transplant survival 5–6 years; age >70 has higher 30-day/1-year mortality (p1258–1259).":["u43"],"palliative-care|Palliative care":["u44"],"copy|Average life expectancy from diagnosis: 14 years for COPD vs 21 years for heart disease (p1262).":["u45"],"copy|Symptom burden in advanced COPD (Table 81-14): dyspnea 94%, pain 54% (p1263); respiratory symptoms (dyspnea, cough, sputum) up to 98% of patients (p1262).":["u46"],"copy|Psychological/sleep symptom burden in advanced COPD: depression >90% (antidepressants, supportive counseling); anxiety >90% (benzodiazepines, behavioral interventions); delirium (antipsychotics + behavioral interventions); sleep disturbance 40% (trazodone/Z-meds, nocturnal O2, CPAP, CBT) (Table 81-14, p1263).":["u47"],"copy|Very severe obstruction (FEV1 <30% predicted or FEV1 Z-score <–2.55) helps identify patients at risk for uncontrolled symptoms from advanced COPD — but FEV1 alone is not sufficient to identify who would benefit from palliative care (p1262).":["u48"],"copy|SPIKES framework for breaking bad news: Setting, Perception, Invitation, Knowledge, Empathy, Strategy/summary (p1264).":["u49"],"recall|Recall":["u50"],"copy|row:Exacerbation severity|TH|Exacerbation severity":["u52"],"copy|row:Exacerbation severity|TH|Antibiotics":["u53"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Mild":["u54"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|None":["u55"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Moderatea,b":["u56"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Macrolide, doxycycline, cephalosporin, amoxicillin/clavulanate, bactrim, fluoroquinolone":["u57"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Severec,d":["u58"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Fluoroquinolone, antipseudomonal penicillin, cephalosporin (third generation), vancomycin (if concern for MRSA)":["u59"],"copy|row:SYMPTOM|TH|SYMPTOM":["u61"],"copy|row:SYMPTOM|TH|PREVALENCE":["u62"],"copy|row:SYMPTOM|TH|THERAPIES":["u63"],"copy|row:SYMPTOM|TH|NOTES":["u64"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Nausea/vomiting":["u65"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Haloperidol":["u66"],"copy|CHAPTER 81s / HAZZARD":["u67"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 81 — Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u68"],"copy|1.":["u69"],"copy|What are the two fundamental age-related flaws in the GOLD spirometric criteria for COPD?":["u70"],"copy|(1) A fixed FEV1/FVC < 0.70 does not distinguish normal age-related airflow limitation from true COPD-related obstruction (often <0.70 in healthy older never-smokers).":["u71"],"copy|(2) FEV1 expressed as %Pred assumes spirometric variability is constant across the adult lifespan, which it is not (p1236, p1243).":["u72"],"copy|2.":["u73"],"copy|At what GLI Z-score threshold is airflow obstruction (LLN) defined, and what does a Z-score of –2.55 signify?":["u74"],"copy|Z-score < –1.64 defines the lower limit of normal (5th percentile). –2.55 corresponds to the 0.5th percentile and is used as a severity threshold (severe FEV1 Z-score < –2.55), and appears as a marker of resistant-organism risk in exacerbations and of risk for uncontrolled symptoms from advanced COPD (p1236, p1243–1244, p1262).":["u75"],"copy|3.":["u76"],"copy|What proportion of people with symptoms of chronic bronchitis, and what proportion with physician-diagnosed emphysema/COPD, have normal spirometry?":["u77"],"copy|Two-thirds of those with chronic-bronchitis symptoms and half of those with physician-diagnosed emphysema/COPD have normal spirometry (no airflow obstruction) — yet remain at risk for poor outcomes (p1235).":["u78"],"copy|4.":["u79"],"copy|What average number of chronic conditions and filled prescriptions per year characterizes a COPD patient?":["u80"],"copy|3.7 chronic conditions and 35 filled prescriptions per year (p1247).":["u81"],"copy|5.":["u82"],"copy|What eosinophil threshold favors adding inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) to bronchodilator therapy, and what eosinophil level increases ICS-associated pneumonia risk?":["u83"],"copy|ICS combined with LABA reduces exacerbations and hospitalizations with absolute eosinophil count ≥300 cells/μL plus high symptom burden and frequent exacerbations (GOLD Group D).":["u84"],"copy|Pneumonia risk with ICS is greatest with eosinophils <100 cells/μL (also older age, frailty, low BMI, high ICS dose); ICS withdrawal can be considered with eosinophils <300 (especially <100) cells/μL (p1253–1254).":["u85"],"copy|(Table 81-7, p1246, and GOLD Figure 3.10 use cells/μL; the prose on p1253 prints cells/mL.)":["u85"],"copy|7.":["u86"],"copy|What peak inspiratory flow (PIF) does a dry powder inhaler (DPI) require, and which device is preferred when PIF is inadequate?":["u87"],"copy|DPIs require PIF > 45 L/min.":["u88"],"copy|A soft-mist inhaler (SMI) needs only a gentle breath, offering an advantage over DPIs and pMDIs for patients with low PIF or poor \"press and breathe\" coordination (p1255).":["u89"],"copy|8.":["u90"],"copy|What MMSE and EXIT-25 cutoffs predict impaired ability to learn inhaler technique?":["u91"],"copy|MMSE < 24, or EXIT-25 ≥ 15 (also inability to draw intersecting pentagons) (p1255).":["u92"],"copy|9.":["u93"],"copy|At what prednisone-equivalent dose does delirium risk rise significantly during COPD exacerbation treatment, and what shorter regimen was shown noninferior to a 7–14 day taper?":["u94"],"copy|Delirium risk rises above 60 mg/day.":["u95"],"copy|A 5-day course of oral prednisone 40 mg/day was noninferior to a conventional 7–14 day taper, with a shorter length of stay (p1236, p1261).":["u96"],"copy|10.":["u97"],"copy|What are the three most common bacterial pathogens in acute COPD exacerbations?":["u98"],"copy|Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, and Streptococcus pneumoniae (p1259, Table 81-12).":["u99"],"copy|11.":["u100"],"copy|What PaO2/SpO2 thresholds indicate daily long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT), and how many hours per day are needed for a survival benefit?":["u101"],"copy|Resting PaO2 ≤ 55 mm Hg or SpO2 ≤ 88% (or PaO2 ≤ 59/SpO2 ≤ 89% with cor pulmonale, pulmonary hypertension, or erythrocytosis).":["u102"],"copy|LTOT worn more than 16 hours per day improves survival (p1256).":["u103"],"copy|12.":["u104"],"copy|What FEV1/DLCO and homogeneity findings make a patient a poor candidate for lung volume reduction surgery (LVRS)?":["u105"],"copy|FEV1 or DLCO < 20% predicted with homogeneous (rather than upper-lobe-predominant) emphysema — higher surgical mortality; should not undergo LVRS (p1258).":["u106"],"copy|13.":["u107"],"copy|What BODE Index score threshold, and what FEV1/DLCO percentage, triggers lung transplant referral consideration?":["u108"],"copy|BODE Index score > 5, and FEV1 and DLCO both < 25% predicted, among other criteria (PaCO2 > 50 mmHg, PaO2 < 60 mmHg, pulmonary hypertension/cor pulmonale, ≥1 hospitalization with acute hypercapnia or ≥3 severe exacerbations in the prior year) (p1258–1259).":["u109"],"copy|14.":["u110"],"copy|How does average life expectancy from diagnosis compare between COPD and heart disease?":["u111"],"copy|14 years for COPD vs 21 years for heart disease (p1262).":["u112"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.1260 · Read source":["u113"],"copy|aModerate exacerbations are accompanied by at least two of the following: increased dyspnea, sputum volume, and sputum purulence—in the absence of respiratory failure. bSevere exacerbations are accompanied by at least two of the following: increased dyspnea, sputum volume, and sputum purulence—concurrent with respiratory failure. cIf risk factors for Pseudomonas, use ciprofloxacin and obtain sputum culture. dIf risk factors for Pseudomonas, use pipercillin-tazobactam or ceftazidime, and obtain sputum culture.":["u114"],"copy|If risk factors for MRSA, check nasal MRSA swab and obtain sputum culture.":["u115"],"copy|2024-Sep Q68":["u116"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.1263 · Read source":["u117"],"copy|2024-Sep Q11":["u118"],"copy|Answer":["u119","u120","u121","u122","u123","u124","u125","u126","u127","u128","u129","u130","u131"],"copy|LVRS candidates: FEV1 <50% predicted, RV >150–200% predicted, upper-lobe-predominant emphysema; For surgical risk, check the original NETT definition: FEV₁ at most 20% predicted, together with either homogeneous emphysema on CT or DLCO at most 20% predicted. This subgroup had excess operative mortality and little functional benefit. Page 1258 groups the criteria differently. NETT high-risk analysis.":["u42"],"copy|In NETT, FEV₁ at most 20% predicted plus either homogeneous emphysema or DLCO at most 20% predicted identified patients at high surgical risk.":["u106"],"copy|The 30-day mortality was 16% after surgery versus 0% with medical treatment.":["u106"],"copy|Page 1258 phrases the criteria differently.":["u106"],"copy|Original NETT analysis.":["u106"],"copy|Hazzard reports COPD as the fourth leading cause of death in the US and projects fifth for disability and third for death worldwide by 2030 (p1235).Current practice (not the book): In CDC's final 2024 US statistics, the broader category chronic lower respiratory diseases, which includes COPD and asthma, ranked fifth. CDC/NCHS, final 2024 mortality.":["u4"],"copy|Table 81-5 reproduces older ATS/ERS categories using FEV1 percent predicted: ≥70%, 50–69% and <50% (p1244).Current practice (not the book): The 2022 ERS/ATS interpretive standard grades impairment by z-score. GOLD's airflow-obstruction grades are a separate framework. ERS/ATS interpretive standard, 2022.":["u16"],"copy|Hazzard describes normal FEV1/FVC with low FEV1 and evidence of inflammatory airways disease as a group at risk of progression and calls this PRISm (p1244).Current practice (not the book): GOLD defines the post-bronchodilator pattern by FEV1/FVC ≥0.70 and FEV1 <80% predicted. Evidence of airway inflammation is not required. GOLD 2027, initial pharmacological treatment, groups B and E.":["u248"],"copy|Skeletal muscle dysfunction in ~30%; sarcopenia in an estimated 22% (up to 63% in nursing-home residents) (p1241). Osteoporosis is a common COPD comorbidity and can further impair mobility (p1241 and Table 81-3, p1242).":["u20"],"copy|LVRS candidates: FEV1 <50% predicted, RV >150–200% predicted, upper-lobe-predominant emphysema. Hazzard says surgical LVRS should be avoided when FEV1 or DLCO is <20% predicted and emphysema is homogeneous because postoperative mortality is higher (p1258).Current practice (not the book): NETT defined high risk as FEV1 ≤20% predicted plus either homogeneous emphysema or DLCO ≤20% predicted. NETT high-risk analysis.":["u42"],"copy|The chapter advises considering osteoporosis prevention for patients receiving long-term ICS.":["u182"],"copy|In the chapter’s GOLD framework, LAMA is generally the first long-acting bronchodilator.":["u249"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): GOLD 2027 recommends LABA plus LAMA as initial therapy for most group B and E patients and permits consideration of initial triple therapy for group E at ≥300 cells/µL.":["u189"],"copy|[":["u192"],"copy|Hazzard advises direct fluorescent antibody testing with or without PCR during influenza season because specific antiviral therapy may be indicated (p1260).":["u251"],"copy|A positive rhinovirus PCR does not exclude bacterial coinfection.":["u200"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): During a winter admission, use a molecular influenza assay when influenza is suspected and start empiric oseltamivir promptly, without waiting for the result.":["u252"],"copy|CDC, influenza antiviral treatment in hospitalized patients":["u253"],"copy|Hazzard reports benefit from ICS/LABA with eosinophils ≥300 cells/mL and high symptom burden plus frequent exacerbations (Group D).":["u84"],"copy|ICS-associated pneumonia risk rises with <100 cells/mL, older age, frailty, low BMI or high dose.":["u85"],"copy|The chapter discusses ICS withdrawal with <300 cells/mL, particularly <100 cells/mL (pp1253–1254). [sic — all three units are printed per mL in the prose; Table 81-7 on p1246 uses cells/µL.]":["u254"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): GOLD expresses these eosinophil counts in cells/µL.":["u255"],"copy|GOLD Figure 3.10.":["u256"],"copy|Hazzard says surgical LVRS should be avoided when FEV1 or DLCO is <20% predicted and emphysema is homogeneous because postoperative mortality is higher (p1258).":["u257"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): In NETT, FEV₁ at most 20% predicted plus either homogeneous emphysema or DLCO at most 20% predicted identified patients at high surgical risk.":["u106"],"copy|CHAPTER 81 / HAZZARD":["u67"],"table-81-13|Table 81-13":["u51"],"table-81-14-nausea-vomiting|Table 81-14: Nausea/vomiting":["u60"],"copy|COPD inhaled treatment: offer a short-acting rescue bronchodilator. Hazzard generally uses a LAMA first for long-acting bronchodilation. In Group D, high symptom burden, frequent exacerbations and blood eosinophils ≥300 cells/mL in the text support adding ICS to LABA (pp1253–1254). [sic — Table 81-7, p1246, uses cells/µL.]Current practice (not the book): GOLD 2026 generally favors LABA/LAMA for symptomatic group B or exacerbation-prone group E patients and considers LABA/LAMA/ICS in group E at eosinophils ≥300 cells/μL. Following exacerbations on LABA/LAMA, ICS benefit is more likely as the eosinophil count rises. Concomitant asthma requires an ICS-containing regimen. GOLD treatment algorithms.":["u24"],"copy|ICS risks in older adults: 1.2–1.6-fold increased pneumonia risk (highest with older age, frailty, low BMI, high dose, eosinophils <100 cells/mL in the text [sic — Table 81-7 uses cells/µL]); consider osteoporosis prevention during long-term treatment, especially given accelerated bone loss above 1000 mcg/day fluticasone-equivalent; subcapsular cataracts are established with systemic steroids, while causation with ICS is less certain; atypical mycobacterial infection (p1253–1254).":["u25"],"copy|ICS with LABA reduces exacerbations and hospitalizations in Group D when eosinophils are ≥300 cells/mL in the text, symptoms are substantial and exacerbations frequent (pp1253–1254). [sic — Table 81-7, p1246, uses cells/µL.]":["u250"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[258,-126],[308,-125],[311,-121],[313,-120],[319,-118],[366,0]]}
